# ============================================================================
# Network Module - Outputs
# ============================================================================
#
# PURPOSE:
#   This file defines output values that the module exposes to the calling
#   module. Outputs allow the root module to access information about network
#   resources created within this module, which can then be used by other
#   modules (like the Databricks module).
#
# KEY CONCEPTS FOR BEGINNERS:
#   - Module outputs: Values exposed from the module to the caller
#   - Accessing outputs: Root modules use module.<name>.<output> syntax
#   - Output dependencies: Outputs can reference any resource created in the module
#   - Information hiding: Only outputs are accessible outside the module
#   - Module composition: Outputs from one module can be inputs to another
#
# TERRAFORM DOCUMENTATION:
#   - Module Outputs: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/outputs
#   - Accessing Module Outputs: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/syntax#accessing-module-output-values
#
# USAGE EXAMPLE:
#   In the root module, you would access outputs like:
#   module "databricks" {
#     source = "./modules/databricks"
#     network_id = module.network.vpc_id  # Pass network output to Databricks module
#   }
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK OUTPUTS
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The VPC/VNet ID that was created or is being used
# This is useful for creating additional resources in the same network
# Format varies by provider:
#   - AWS: vpc-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#   - Azure: /subscriptions/.../resourceGroups/.../providers/Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/...
#   - GCP: projects/PROJECT_ID/global/networks/VPC_NAME
output "vpc_id" {
  description = "VPC/VNet ID - use this to create additional resources in the same network"
  value       = "Configured in main.tf"
  
  # Note: In a complete implementation, this would reference the actual network resource:
  # AWS: value = aws_vpc.this.id
  # Azure: value = azurerm_virtual_network.this.id
  # GCP: value = google_compute_network.this.id
  #
  # This output is useful for:
  # - Passing to other modules (like Databricks module)
  # - Creating additional resources in the same network
  # - Network troubleshooting and documentation
  # - Connecting other Terraform configurations to this network
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: Additional Outputs
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In a complete module implementation, you would also have outputs for:
# - subnet_ids: List of subnet IDs (for Databricks network configuration)
# - public_subnet_ids: List of public subnet IDs
# - private_subnet_ids: List of private subnet IDs
# - security_group_id: Security group/NSG ID (for Databricks)
# - internet_gateway_id: Internet gateway ID (AWS)
# - nat_gateway_id: NAT gateway ID (if created)
# - route_table_ids: Route table IDs
# - vpc_cidr: VPC CIDR block (for reference)
# - And other provider-specific network identifiers
